  "error_budget": {
    "measured_real": "NCCL 2.32.6 chose our mlx5_0 (NET/IB, no Socket fallback); its own kernels ran on the real GPU; both ranks posted real verbs WRs and the payload bytes crossed the modelled wire (counters: post_send_calls > 0, bytes_written > 0, ring records in/out > 0).",
    "modelled": "The wire: libibverbs is a shim and the 'fabric' is a MAP_SHARED byte ring per rank pair, with modelled bandwidth/latency applied to completion readiness. The GPU identity is one real device seen through N aliases. Delivery is asynchronous (pump thread); the pump interval (default 50 us) is added real latency, not modelled wire time.",
    "not_modelled": "No physical NIC, no DMA/PCIe transfer, no GDR/dmabuf registration (7q3w.6), no second GPU, and therefore no B300 compute time: a collective's COMPUTE time is this 5090's, while its TRANSPORT time is the D1 model. No 8-way collective and no training step here; no performance claim (that is gpu-sim-ae2v).",
    "model_defect_found_and_fixed": "Inbound records used to be delivered only inside poll_cq, so a sender spinning on the peer-written host FIFO could never be progressed (rank1 wedged with rings out=49 in=48). The pump thread models the NIC writing memory on its own; phase C is that control."
  },
